    <!-- ================================================== -->
    <section class="section" id="overview">
      <div class="section-header"><h3>How the service works</h3></div>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>
          TFSrun storage is an <strong>S3-compatible object store</strong> powered by MinIO. Any application
          that speaks the S3 API can connect to it — the official
          <code>@aws-sdk/client-s3</code> (AWS SDK v3 for JavaScript) works out of the box because
          MinIO implements the same protocol. The portal itself uses this exact SDK internally, and the
          code below mirrors the working reference app (<code>test3-app</code>) uploaded with this project.
        </p>
        <p>
          The flow is always the same: your backend holds a <strong>bucket</strong> plus an
          <strong>access key / secret key</strong> pair, creates one <code>S3Client</code>, and then sends
          commands (<code>PutObjectCommand</code>, <code>ListObjectsV2Command</code>,
          <code>GetObjectCommand</code>, <code>DeleteObjectCommand</code>) through it.
          Files are called <em>objects</em> and are addressed by a <em>key</em> (their name inside the bucket).
        </p>
        <p class="muted small"><strong>Important:</strong> browser-side JavaScript must never hold these credentials. Always talk to S3 from a backend (Node.js, Python, etc.) and expose your own API routes to the browser, exactly like the reference app does.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ================================================== -->
    <section class="section" id="credentials">
      <div class="section-header"><h3>Step 1 — Get your connection details</h3></div>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>Open the <a href="/student/s3">Storage workspace</a> and do two things:</p>
        <ol class="docs-list">
          <li><strong>Create (or select) a bucket.</strong> Every account gets a default
            <code>&lt;username&gt;-workspace</code> bucket on first visit. You can create more with the
            “Create bucket” button. Your default bucket here is:
            <% if (defaultBucket) { %><code><%= defaultBucket %></code><% } else { %><code>&lt;username&gt;-workspace</code><% } %>
          </li>
          <li><strong>Create an application key.</strong> Click <em>Create app key</em> on a bucket row.
            The portal generates a MinIO access key / secret key pair scoped to that bucket and shows them
            <strong>once</strong> in the “Application connection details” panel. Save the secret immediately —
            it is never displayed again. If you lose it, delete the key and create a new one.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>That panel gives you the five values every integration needs:</p>
        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Detail</th><th>Example</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Endpoint</strong></td><td><code><%= endpoint %></code></td><td>Base URL of the MinIO S3 API</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Bucket</strong></td><td><% if (defaultBucket) { %><code><%= defaultBucket %></code><% } else { %><code>yourname-workspace</code><% } %></td><td>The folder-like container your files live in</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Region</strong></td><td><code><%= region %></code></td><td>S3 region string (any value works with MinIO, keep it consistent)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Access key</strong></td><td><code>e.g. aBc123XyZ...</code></td><td>Public identifier of your app key (like a username)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Secret key</strong></td><td><code>shown only once</code></td><td>The signing secret (like a password) — keep it private</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="muted small">The portal never shows access keys of other students and MinIO enforces that a key can only touch the bucket it was scoped to.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ================================================== -->
    <section class="section" id="install">
      <div class="section-header"><h3>Step 2 — Install the SDK</h3></div>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>The reference app uses <code>@aws-sdk/client-s3</code> (AWS SDK v3) together with
          <code>multer</code> for receiving files in an Express server and <code>dotenv</code> for the config:</p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>npm install @aws-sdk/client-s3 multer dotenv express</code></pre>
        <p class="muted small">Works the same way from other languages: any S3 client (boto3 for Python, aws-sdk-go, MinIO SDKs…) will connect with the same five details. This guide stays in JavaScript/Node.js like the reference app.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ================================================== -->
    <section class="section" id="client">
      <div class="section-header"><h3>Step 3 — Create the S3 client</h3></div>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>Put your five details into a <code>.env</code> file in your project root:</p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>S3_ENDPOINT=<%= endpoint %>
S3_REGION=<%= region %>
S3_BUCKET=<% if (defaultBucket) { %><%= defaultBucket %><% } else { %>yourname-workspace<% } %>
S3_ACCESS_KEY=your-access-key-from-the-portal
S3_SECRET_KEY=your-secret-key-from-the-portal</code></pre>
        <p>Then create one client and reuse it for every request. Note <code>forcePathStyle: true</code> — it is <strong>required</strong> for MinIO:</p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>require("dotenv").config();

const { S3Client } = require("@aws-sdk/client-s3");

const s3 = new S3Client({
    endpoint: process.env.S3_ENDPOINT,
    region: process.env.S3_REGION,

    credentials: {
        accessKeyId: process.env.S3_ACCESS_KEY,
        secretAccessKey: process.env.S3_SECRET_KEY,
    },

    // Required for MinIO-style endpoints:
    // addresses the bucket in the URL path (http://host/bucket)
    // instead of a subdomain (bucket.http://host).
    forcePathStyle: true,
});</code></pre>
        <div class="alert info"><strong>Connection checklist.</strong> If a request ever fails, 99% of the time it is one of these: wrong endpoint (must include <code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code>), missing <code>forcePathStyle: true</code>, access/secret key mismatch, or the key was scoped to a different bucket than the one you are addressing.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ================================================== -->
    <section class="section" id="test">
      <div class="section-header"><h3>Step 4 — Test the connection</h3></div>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>Before building features, verify the credentials with <code>HeadBucketCommand</code> — the cheapest
          possible call. This is exactly what the reference app's “Test S3 Connection” button does:</p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const { HeadBucketCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-s3");

app.get("/api/test", async (req, res) => {
    try {
        await s3.send(
            new HeadBucketCommand({ Bucket: process.env.S3_BUCKET })
        );

        res.json({
            success: true,
            message: "Successfully connected to S3 bucket",
            bucket: process.env.S3_BUCKET,
        });

    } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
        res.status(500).json({ success: false, message: error.message });
    }
});</code></pre>
        <p class="muted small"><code>200 OK</code> from HeadBucket means endpoint, region, credentials, and bucket are all correct in one shot.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ================================================== -->
    <section class="section" id="upload">
      <div class="section-header"><h3>Step 5 — Upload a file</h3></div>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>The browser POSTs a <code>multipart/form-data</code> form to your backend; <code>multer</code>
          receives it, and the buffer is put into the bucket with <code>PutObjectCommand</code>:</p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const multer = require("multer");
const { PutObjectCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-s3");

// Store the incoming file in memory (use diskStorage for very large files)
const upload = multer({ storage: multer.memoryStorage() });

app.post("/api/upload", upload.single("file"), async (req, res) => {
    try {
        if (!req.file) {
            return res.status(400).json({ success: false, message: "No file selected" });
        }

        await s3.send(
            new PutObjectCommand({
                Bucket: process.env.S3_BUCKET,
                Key: req.file.originalname,      // the name the file gets in the bucket
                Body: req.file.buffer,           // the file content
                ContentType: req.file.mimetype,  // so downloads/browsers know the type
            })
        );

        res.json({
            success: true,
            message: "File uploaded successfully",
            fileName: req.file.originalname,
        });

    } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
        res.status(500).json({ success: false, message: error.message });
    }
});</code></pre>
        <p>From the browser, a plain <code>FormData</code> upload is all it takes:</p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>&lt;input type="file" id="fileInput"&gt;
&lt;button onclick="uploadFile()"&gt;Upload File&lt;/button&gt;

&lt;script&gt;
    async function uploadFile() {
        const input = document.getElementById("fileInput");
        if (!input.files.length) return;

        const formData = new FormData();
        formData.append("file", input.files[0]);   // field name must be "file"

        const response = await fetch("/api/upload", {
            method: "POST",
            body: formData,
        });

        const data = await response.json();
        console.log(data.success ? "Uploaded: " + data.fileName : data.message);
    }
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
        <p class="muted small">The field name in <code>formData.append("file", …)</code> must match <code>upload.single("file")</code>. The TFSrun portal itself streams uploads through a temp file on disk instead of memory, which removes any size ceiling — copy that pattern if you expect large files.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ================================================== -->
    <section class="section" id="more-operations">
      <div class="section-header"><h3>Step 6 — List, download, and delete files</h3></div>
      <div class="panel">
        <p><strong>List objects</strong> in the bucket:</p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const { ListObjectsV2Command } = require("@aws-sdk/client-s3");

app.get("/api/files", async (req, res) => {
    try {
        const response = await s3.send(
            new ListObjectsV2Command({ Bucket: process.env.S3_BUCKET })
        );

        const files = (response.Contents || []).map((file) => ({
            key: file.Key,
            size: file.Size,
            lastModified: file.LastModified,
        }));

        res.json({ success: true, files });

    } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
        res.status(500).json({ success: false, message: error.message });
    }
});</code></pre>
        <p><strong>Download</strong> an object and stream it back to the browser:</p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const { GetObjectCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-s3");

app.get("/api/files/:key/download", async (req, res) => {
    try {
        const response = await s3.send(
            new GetObjectCommand({
                Bucket: process.env.S3_BUCKET,
                Key: req.params.key,
            })
        );

        res.setHeader("Content-Type", response.ContentType || "application/octet-stream");
        res.setHeader("Content-Disposition", `attachment; filename="${req.params.key}"`);
        response.Body.pipe(res);   // Body is a readable stream

    } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
        res.status(404).send("File not found.");
    }
});</code></pre>
        <p><strong>Delete</strong> an object:</p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const { DeleteObjectCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-s3");

app.delete("/api/files/:key", async (req, res) => {
    try {
        await s3.send(
            new DeleteObjectCommand({
                Bucket: process.env.S3_BUCKET,
                Key: req.params.key,
            })
        );

        res.json({ success: true });

    } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
        res.status(500).json({ success: false, message: error.message });
    }
});</code></pre>
        <p class="muted small"><strong>Keys</strong> can contain slashes to fake folders, e.g. <code>projects/2026/report.pdf</code>. Listing supports <code>Prefix</code> and <code>Delimiter</code> options, and pages results — pass <code>ContinuationToken</code> for more than 1000 objects.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ================================================== -->
    <section class="section" id="full-example">
      <div class="section-header"><h3>Complete minimal example (copy-paste)</h3></div>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>A whole working server — the same skeleton as the reference <code>test3-app/server.js</code>:</p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>require("dotenv").config();

const express = require("express");
const multer = require("multer");
const {
    S3Client,
    PutObjectCommand,
    ListObjectsV2Command,
    GetObjectCommand,
    DeleteObjectCommand,
    HeadBucketCommand,
} = require("@aws-sdk/client-s3");

const app = express();
const BUCKET = process.env.S3_BUCKET;

const s3 = new S3Client({
    endpoint: process.env.S3_ENDPOINT,
    region: process.env.S3_REGION,
    credentials: {
        accessKeyId: process.env.S3_ACCESS_KEY,
        secretAccessKey: process.env.S3_SECRET_KEY,
    },
    forcePathStyle: true,
});

const upload = multer({ storage: multer.memoryStorage() });

app.use(express.json());
app.use(express.static("public"));

// 1. Health check
app.get("/api/test", async (_req, res) => {
    try {
        await s3.send(new HeadBucketCommand({ Bucket: BUCKET }));
        res.json({ success: true, message: "Connected", bucket: BUCKET });
    } catch (error) {
        res.status(500).json({ success: false, message: error.message });
    }
});

// 2. Upload
app.post("/api/upload", upload.single("file"), async (req, res) => {
    try {
        if (!req.file) {
            return res.status(400).json({ success: false, message: "No file selected" });
        }
        await s3.send(new PutObjectCommand({
            Bucket: BUCKET,
            Key: req.file.originalname,
            Body: req.file.buffer,
            ContentType: req.file.mimetype,
        }));
        res.json({ success: true, fileName: req.file.originalname });
    } catch (error) {
        res.status(500).json({ success: false, message: error.message });
    }
});

// 3. List
app.get("/api/files", async (_req, res) => {
    try {
        const out = await s3.send(new ListObjectsV2Command({ Bucket: BUCKET }));
        res.json({
            success: true,
            files: (out.Contents || []).map(f => ({
                key: f.Key, size: f.Size, lastModified: f.LastModified,
            })),
        });
    } catch (error) {
        res.status(500).json({ success: false, message: error.message });
    }
});

// 4. Download
app.get("/api/files/:key/download", async (req, res) => {
    try {
        const out = await s3.send(new GetObjectCommand({
            Bucket: BUCKET, Key: req.params.key,
        }));
        res.setHeader("Content-Type", out.ContentType || "application/octet-stream");
        res.setHeader("Content-Disposition", `attachment; filename="${req.params.key}"`);
        out.Body.pipe(res);
    } catch (error) {
        res.status(404).send("File not found.");
    }
});

// 5. Delete
app.delete("/api/files/:key", async (req, res) => {
    try {
        await s3.send(new DeleteObjectCommand({ Bucket: BUCKET, Key: req.params.key }));
        res.json({ success: true });
    } catch (error) {
        res.status(500).json({ success: false, message: error.message });
    }
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log("Server running at http://localhost:3000");
});</code></pre>
      </div>
    </section>

